## Conditions Governing the Grant of Bail in Offences Under the Prevention of Money Laundering Act (PMLA)

### I. Statutory Framework — Section 45(1) of PMLA

Section 45(1) of the PMLA mandates that offences under the Act are **cognizable and non-bailable**. Notwithstanding anything in the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973, no person accused of an offence under PMLA shall be released on bail or on his own bond unless the following **two conditions** are satisfied:

- **(i)** The Public Prosecutor has been given an opportunity to oppose the application for such release; and
- **(ii)** Where the Public Prosecutor opposes the application, the court is satisfied that there are **reasonable grounds for believing** that the accused is **not guilty** of the offence and is **not likely to commit any offence while on bail**.

### II. Exceptions — Provisos Under Section 45(1)

The following categories of persons may be released on bail **if the Special Court so directs**, even without satisfying the above conditions:

- A person **under the age of sixteen years**;
- A **woman**;
- A **sick or infirm** person;
- A person accused either on his own or along with co-accused of **money-laundering a sum of less than one crore rupees**.

### III. Judicial Interpretation — Conditions Are Restrictive, Not Absolute

The Supreme Court in **Prem Prakash v. Union of India (28 August 2024)**, citing **Vijay Madanlal Choudhary and Ors.**, held that while Section 45 restricts the right of the accused to bail, the conditions imposed **do not constitute absolute restraint** on the grant of bail. The discretion vests in the court and is **judicial, not arbitrary or irrational**.

The Court further affirmed, referencing **Manish Sisodia (II) v. Directorate of Enforcement**, that the governing principle even under PMLA remains: **"Bail is the Rule and Jail is the Exception."** Section 45 merely prescribes conditions that must be satisfied — it does not create a blanket prohibition.

### IV. General Parameters Considered by Courts

While adjudicating bail applications under PMLA, courts also consider the broader parameters of bail jurisprudence, including:

- The **nature and gravity** of the accusation;
- The **role attributed** to the accused;
- **Criminal antecedents** of the accused;
- The **likelihood of tampering** with evidence or witnesses;
- The **risk of abscondence** or fleeing from justice;
- The **likelihood of repeating** the offence; and
- The **overall impact on society** and the desirability of releasing the accused on bail.

### V. Context of Prolonged Custody

The Supreme Court has also recognized that even under special enactments with rigorous bail conditions like PMLA, courts may consider the **period of custody already suffered** and the **likely period for completion of trial** as relevant factors when deciding on bail, drawing a parallel with the approach taken under NDPS and UAPA jurisprudence.
